VALUES ('84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 'cpt-practice-1', 'CPT Coding Proficiency Practice Paper 1', 'cpt', ARRAY['Evaluation & Management', 'Anesthesia', 'Surgery', 'Radiology', 'Pathology & Laboratory', 'Medicine', 'Modifiers', 'NCCI', 'Global Surgical Package']::TEXT[], 100, 'advanced', true, 1, 150);

-- ── Section: Evaluation & Management (20 questions) — Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines (2021+ office/outpatient E/M revisions: medical decision making and time-based leveling); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook's code descriptions, and not an official certification body's exam content. ──

VALUES ('ec3a760d-1098-4acd-9985-22683b3d88ec', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 1, 'Under the 2021+ CPT office/outpatient E/M guidelines, the level of an E/M service may be selected based on which of the following, at the physician''s choice?');
VALUES ('ec3a760d-1098-4acd-9985-22683b3d88ec', '63186d2f-5b0e-4455-ac56-aead6aa6394b', 'The 2021 CPT overhaul of office/outpatient E/M codes made time OR medical decision making the two permissible leveling methods — history/exam remain clinically relevant but are no longer the code-level-determining factors they were under the prior 1995/1997 guidelines for these codes.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines (2021+ office/outpatient E/M revisions: medical decision making and time-based leveling); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook''s code descriptions, and not an official certification body''s exam content.');

VALUES ('190fe468-9dea-4fbd-b83f-f10197536b77', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 2, 'Under current E/M medical decision making (MDM) leveling, which of the following is one of the three MDM elements considered?');
VALUES ('190fe468-9dea-4fbd-b83f-f10197536b77', 'e2a998d4-603f-4d09-9468-9907652b0cd9', 'The three MDM elements are: (1) number and complexity of problems addressed, (2) amount/complexity of data reviewed and analyzed, and (3) risk of complications/morbidity/mortality of patient management — insurance type, time of day, and specialty are not MDM elements.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines (2021+ office/outpatient E/M revisions: medical decision making and time-based leveling); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook''s code descriptions, and not an official certification body''s exam content.');

VALUES ('4c0c1976-9396-4503-8c5f-56ec019c93b7', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 3, 'When time is used to select an E/M code under current guidelines, which of the following is included in ''total time on the date of the encounter''?');
VALUES ('4c0c1976-9396-4503-8c5f-56ec019c93b7', '9cb95db0-9533-4505-b06d-08125633ece9', 'Current E/M time-based guidelines count total physician/QHP time on the date of the encounter, including non-face-to-face work like chart review and documentation — a significant change from the old ''face-to-face time'' standard, and clinical staff/waiting-room time is not counted.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines (2021+ office/outpatient E/M revisions: medical decision making and time-based leveling); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook''s code descriptions, and not an official certification body''s exam content.');

VALUES ('1fe139b2-628e-4062-9fc1-e45fc797d18a', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 4, 'A ''new patient'' for E/M coding purposes is generally defined as:');
VALUES ('1fe139b2-628e-4062-9fc1-e45fc797d18a', 'cf7c20bc-d511-4cc9-aad1-db946c7d2a70', 'The new-vs-established patient distinction hinges on whether the same physician or same-specialty/subspecialty group-practice colleague has provided a professional service within the prior three years — not the patient''s medical history, age, or insurance status.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines (2021+ office/outpatient E/M revisions: medical decision making and time-based leveling); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook''s code descriptions, and not an official certification body''s exam content.');

VALUES ('96c36a9e-1610-4e2c-a7b2-c58e2908870c', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 5, 'Which of the following best describes ''medically appropriate history and/or examination,'' as referenced in current office/outpatient E/M guidelines?');
VALUES ('96c36a9e-1610-4e2c-a7b2-c58e2908870c', '31aaace1-fd2e-4179-b158-382cf2ae0bba', 'Post-2021, history/exam remain clinically necessary and should be documented as medically appropriate, but they no longer drive code-level selection via a rigid point/bullet system — that role now belongs to time or MDM.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines (2021+ office/outpatient E/M revisions: medical decision making and time-based leveling); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook''s code descriptions, and not an official certification body''s exam content.');

VALUES ('6d8d5a94-6848-4fc9-8e31-d99c9fe636d1', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 6, 'Which of the following would generally be classified as a ''straightforward'' level of medical decision making?');
VALUES ('6d8d5a94-6848-4fc9-8e31-d99c9fe636d1', 'f3dac8da-5801-4925-ad4e-1bc016b8b908', 'Straightforward MDM corresponds to the lowest complexity tier: a minor/self-limited problem, minimal-to-no data review, and minimal risk — the other options describe progressively higher MDM complexity tiers (moderate/high).', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines (2021+ office/outpatient E/M revisions: medical decision making and time-based leveling); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook''s code descriptions, and not an official certification body''s exam content.');

VALUES ('1104ae83-0e45-4088-8db0-1cfcc2278aa2', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 7, 'A consultation service, when reported, generally requires which of the following to be documented, per standard CPT consultation guidance principles (where consultation codes are still recognized by a payer)?');
VALUES ('1104ae83-0e45-4088-8db0-1cfcc2278aa2', '03c4350a-45cd-4e49-9d55-118e15dd5723', 'The traditional three-part consultation requirement — a request, a reason, and a report back to the requesting provider — remains the core documentation concept for consultation coding, even though CMS itself no longer recognizes/pays consultation codes under Medicare (a nuance worth knowing, though many commercial payers still do).', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines (2021+ office/outpatient E/M revisions: medical decision making and time-based leveling); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook''s code descriptions, and not an official certification body''s exam content.');

VALUES ('8203b657-adc6-4ff9-a812-bbbb2f8ed860', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 8, 'Which of the following best describes the difference between a ''problem-oriented'' E/M visit and a ''preventive medicine'' E/M visit, from a coding standpoint?');
VALUES ('8203b657-adc6-4ff9-a812-bbbb2f8ed860', '227289ba-5e36-4123-8578-a937a0d8eafb', 'Preventive medicine E/M codes are a structurally separate code family (selected by patient age, not MDM/time) from problem-oriented office/outpatient E/M codes — and when a significant, separately identifiable problem is also addressed during a preventive visit, both may be reported together with an appropriate modifier.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines (2021+ office/outpatient E/M revisions: medical decision making and time-based leveling); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook''s code descriptions, and not an official certification body''s exam content.');

VALUES ('436d0c93-7a6c-468a-8171-87b8f1e20d03', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 9, 'When a significant, separately identifiable problem-oriented E/M service is provided during the same visit as a preventive medicine service, correct coding generally requires:');
VALUES ('436d0c93-7a6c-468a-8171-87b8f1e20d03', '83ee2e18-9db0-4cb0-a11c-043f47f1926b', 'This is one of modifier 25''s most common legitimate uses — appending it to the E/M code being reported in addition to another same-day service (here, the preventive visit) to indicate the E/M work was significant and separately identifiable, not routine/inherent to the other service.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines (2021+ office/outpatient E/M revisions: medical decision making and time-based leveling); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook''s code descriptions, and not an official certification body''s exam content.');

VALUES ('07862ab5-e319-44e9-8308-6844e55b5098', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 10, 'Prolonged services codes (add-on codes used alongside a base E/M code) are generally used to report:');
VALUES ('07862ab5-e319-44e9-8308-6844e55b5098', 'd8e4680f-3b10-432a-8735-1708ffcf588b', 'Prolonged services add-on codes specifically capture extra physician/QHP time beyond a base E/M code''s typical time, when time is the basis for the encounter''s leveling and a defined additional-time threshold is met — they don''t substitute for MDM-based leveling and aren''t for staff time.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines (2021+ office/outpatient E/M revisions: medical decision making and time-based leveling); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook''s code descriptions, and not an official certification body''s exam content.');

VALUES ('5f01fb68-854d-4ced-9b38-ffbaf26a285a', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 11, 'Which of the following best describes an E/M ''add-on code'' generally, as a category (not specific to prolonged services)?');
VALUES ('5f01fb68-854d-4ced-9b38-ffbaf26a285a', 'd85941a6-6963-40f8-b891-dbd37d526bfa', 'Add-on codes (a general CPT convention, denoted by a ''+'' symbol in the codebook) are never reported alone — they describe additional work performed in conjunction with, and only reportable alongside, a specified primary procedure/service.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines (2021+ office/outpatient E/M revisions: medical decision making and time-based leveling); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook''s code descriptions, and not an official certification body''s exam content.');

VALUES ('225e24a9-615c-41fc-b169-1bc87e745e63', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 12, 'Which of the following is the most accurate description of ''chief complaint'' in the context of an E/M encounter, even though it is no longer a scored element for leveling purposes?');
VALUES ('225e24a9-615c-41fc-b169-1bc87e745e63', '31f3130d-66a9-45c8-a167-e8d69a0fa4dc', 'Chief complaint remains a fundamental, expected documentation element (establishing medical necessity for the visit) even though it is no longer directly scored for E/M leveling purposes under the current MDM/time-based system.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines (2021+ office/outpatient E/M revisions: medical decision making and time-based leveling); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook''s code descriptions, and not an official certification body''s exam content.');

VALUES ('2123b173-d3e5-4d2a-a5ab-17223a061c95', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 13, 'For emergency department E/M codes, which of the following is true regarding new vs. established patient distinctions?');
VALUES ('2123b173-d3e5-4d2a-a5ab-17223a061c95', '670cbc30-3fee-4b9c-934f-190288247640', 'A well-known ED-specific E/M coding distinction: unlike office/outpatient E/M codes, ED E/M codes make no new-vs-established patient distinction at all — the same code range applies to every ED patient regardless of prior encounter history.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines (2021+ office/outpatient E/M revisions: medical decision making and time-based leveling); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook''s code descriptions, and not an official certification body''s exam content.');

VALUES ('e246e936-a1ec-47cb-8f33-2779105e7d6a', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 14, 'Which of the following best reflects the concept of ''medical necessity'' as it applies broadly across E/M (and other) coding, not tied to any single payer''s specific policy?');
VALUES ('e246e936-a1ec-47cb-8f33-2779105e7d6a', 'eb3cf010-2f79-4e95-a235-da09bc76025b', 'Medical necessity is a foundational coding/compliance principle across all code types (not payer-specific jargon) — code selection should reflect what was clinically necessary and actually performed/reasonable for the presentation, not simply the maximum documentable level.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines (2021+ office/outpatient E/M revisions: medical decision making and time-based leveling); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook''s code descriptions, and not an official certification body''s exam content.');

VALUES ('b4d1175e-e428-4824-b739-f02535563c7f', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 15, 'Under current MDM guidelines, which of the following would most likely be considered when assessing the ''amount and/or complexity of data to be reviewed and analyzed'' element?');
VALUES ('b4d1175e-e428-4824-b739-f02535563c7f', '61ac7962-e85f-42ee-a3ef-96f6d91e996b', 'The data element of MDM specifically credits activities like ordering/reviewing tests, reviewing external records, obtaining history from an independent historian, and independent interpretation of tests — not factors like provider tenure or EHR cosmetics.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines (2021+ office/outpatient E/M revisions: medical decision making and time-based leveling); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook''s code descriptions, and not an official certification body''s exam content.');

VALUES ('696bd637-29fa-4f09-b382-e2625a60ff48', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 16, 'Which best describes the general purpose of Category II CPT codes?');
VALUES ('696bd637-29fa-4f09-b382-e2625a60ff48', 'a455ab39-5960-4278-bafe-941448f64ab0', 'Category II codes are supplemental, non-reimbursed codes specifically designed to support quality/performance measurement reporting — distinct from both Category I (standard billable procedures/services) and Category III (emerging technology) codes.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines (2021+ office/outpatient E/M revisions: medical decision making and time-based leveling); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook''s code descriptions, and not an official certification body''s exam content.');

VALUES ('8d7d5803-2dbe-4e3f-b622-c19423cc7877', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 17, 'Which best describes the general purpose of Category III CPT codes?');
VALUES ('8d7d5803-2dbe-4e3f-b622-c19423cc7877', 'f63fdd13-f451-42a7-9805-3279abffb8a8', 'Category III codes are temporary tracking codes for new/emerging technology and procedures — they may eventually be replaced by a Category I code once a procedure becomes widespread and well-documented, or may be retired if the technology doesn''t gain traction.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines (2021+ office/outpatient E/M revisions: medical decision making and time-based leveling); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook''s code descriptions, and not an official certification body''s exam content.');

VALUES ('a168f0d4-2c44-4610-b904-2471bae70e11', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 18, 'Which of the following best describes ''time spent on independent interpretation of tests'' as a data element that can contribute to MDM complexity, distinguishing it from simply ordering a test?');
VALUES ('a168f0d4-2c44-4610-b904-2471bae70e11', '26693c37-0ecd-4039-9636-54464ee960cb', 'Current MDM data-element guidance distinguishes between simply ordering/reviewing a result and personally, independently interpreting a test — the latter reflects greater cognitive work and is weighted accordingly within the data complexity element.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines (2021+ office/outpatient E/M revisions: medical decision making and time-based leveling); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook''s code descriptions, and not an official certification body''s exam content.');

VALUES ('c906df50-fea0-4c27-aedd-ae8d7b746545', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 19, 'Which of the following best reflects the ''risk of complications and/or morbidity or mortality of patient management'' MDM element?');
VALUES ('c906df50-fea0-4c27-aedd-ae8d7b746545', '3ae43caf-d76b-499e-bdbb-0284ff1e3670', 'The risk element specifically evaluates the risk of the MANAGEMENT choices made (e.g., starting/monitoring drug therapy with risk of toxicity, deciding on hospitalization, considering surgical risk) — not just an abstract severity rating of the diagnosis alone.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines (2021+ office/outpatient E/M revisions: medical decision making and time-based leveling); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook''s code descriptions, and not an official certification body''s exam content.');

VALUES ('811b56c6-d22d-4712-8f94-13e6219dfbf7', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 20, 'Which of the following best describes why a properly leveled E/M code should be based on the encounter as actually documented, rather than the physician''s typical or average visit complexity for that type of patient?');
VALUES ('811b56c6-d22d-4712-8f94-13e6219dfbf7', '2fa561c4-72d7-4932-9b21-2142acdb116d', 'E/M coding accuracy (and compliance) depends on leveling each specific encounter based on its own actual documented time/MDM — defaulting to a ''typical'' level for a given patient type, rather than what actually happened at that visit, is a recognized compliance risk pattern (both over- and under-coding).', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines (2021+ office/outpatient E/M revisions: medical decision making and time-based leveling); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook''s code descriptions, and not an official certification body''s exam content.');

-- ── Section: Anesthesia (10 questions) — Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions (base units, time units, physical status modifiers, qualifying circumstances); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('ad93be73-a7f5-4e26-a541-654022b9a343', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 21, 'CPT anesthesia codes are typically reimbursed based on a formula generally involving which of the following components?');
VALUES ('ad93be73-a7f5-4e26-a541-654022b9a343', '03211ce5-707f-4dfd-b16e-9c8aa255a514', 'The base-unit-plus-time-unit formula (frequently with an added conversion factor and possible modifying units) is the foundational anesthesia payment/coding concept distinguishing it from most other CPT sections'' flat per-procedure valuation.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions (base units, time units, physical status modifiers, qualifying circumstances); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('4efa9c8d-cb5a-4b55-a22d-d821706d6621', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 22, 'Physical status modifiers in CPT anesthesia coding (e.g., P1 through P6) are used to indicate:');
VALUES ('4efa9c8d-cb5a-4b55-a22d-d821706d6621', '488ec3b7-835b-4840-9f41-b06d5ef06520', 'P1–P6 physical status modifiers classify the patient''s health status (P1 = normal healthy patient, escalating in severity, with P6 specifically for a declared brain-dead organ donor) — they reflect patient condition, not technique, provider credentials, or facility status.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions (base units, time units, physical status modifiers, qualifying circumstances); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('2341b999-7f92-411b-a4d2-99c0989b457f', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 23, '''Qualifying circumstances'' add-on codes in CPT anesthesia coding are used to report which of the following?');
VALUES ('2341b999-7f92-411b-a4d2-99c0989b457f', 'a131bd9c-0b51-4421-b31e-b27f387b3352', 'Qualifying circumstances codes are add-on codes specifically for anesthesia complications like extremes of age or emergency conditions — reported in addition to (never instead of) the primary anesthesia procedure code.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions (base units, time units, physical status modifiers, qualifying circumstances); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('d1ce42c8-46a2-43cc-b2b9-1d42aa24ee38', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 24, 'Which of the following best describes ''monitored anesthesia care'' (MAC), as distinguished from general anesthesia, in CPT anesthesia coding terms?');
VALUES ('d1ce42c8-46a2-43cc-b2b9-1d42aa24ee38', '77b0cabd-8f9f-4bc9-9836-cb75c77d49e8', 'MAC is a distinct anesthesia service type (sedation/monitoring without necessarily inducing full unconsciousness) with its own coding conventions, distinguishable from general anesthesia both clinically and, in some coding/billing contexts, by specific modifiers.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions (base units, time units, physical status modifiers, qualifying circumstances); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('8df4cb42-6a0b-4683-a748-ea75f539485d', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 25, 'When the same anesthesiologist provides anesthesia services for two surgical procedures performed during the same operative session on the same patient, general coding guidance directs:');
VALUES ('8df4cb42-6a0b-4683-a748-ea75f539485d', '9830b112-952c-434f-ac07-ffb3033ebe07', 'Standard anesthesia coding convention for multiple procedures in the same operative session uses the anesthesia code carrying the highest base unit value, with time calculated for the entire combined session, rather than stacking separate full anesthesia charges for each procedure.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions (base units, time units, physical status modifiers, qualifying circumstances); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('de2774cc-74bf-4d6c-97c1-2a1183f68c0f', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 26, 'Anesthesia ''time'' for CPT coding/billing purposes generally begins when:');
VALUES ('de2774cc-74bf-4d6c-97c1-2a1183f68c0f', 'd28011bb-b8a4-44dc-98b2-fe7c2167c67a', 'Anesthesia time is a specific, defined concept starting with anesthesia preparation (not surgical incision) and ending when the patient can be safely transferred to postoperative care — distinct from both surgical time and total facility time.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions (base units, time units, physical status modifiers, qualifying circumstances); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('b07dd18e-f3c4-4dbd-8f8c-3baa750f890d', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 27, 'Which of the following would generally be considered included in the base anesthesia code (not separately reportable) for a routine case with no complications?');
VALUES ('b07dd18e-f3c4-4dbd-8f8c-3baa750f890d', '0448e628-0a85-4589-a910-eda69c5f0533', 'Routine, usual pre/post-anesthesia visits and incidental fluid/blood administration are bundled into the base anesthesia code — this bundling concept parallels the global surgical package idea used elsewhere in CPT.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions (base units, time units, physical status modifiers, qualifying circumstances); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('b28af0d9-ce24-4cd5-b436-30a104ef650e', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 28, 'Which of the following best describes ''moderate sedation'' (conscious sedation) CPT coding, as distinguished from general anesthesia or MAC provided by a separate anesthesia provider?');
VALUES ('b28af0d9-ce24-4cd5-b436-30a104ef650e', '12892496-2b52-4d0b-aa3b-9174dd05df10', 'Moderate sedation has its own distinct CPT code family (with codes distinguishing the performing-physician-provided scenario from an independent trained observer scenario), separate from the anesthesia section''s general anesthesia/MAC codes reported by a distinct anesthesia provider.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions (base units, time units, physical status modifiers, qualifying circumstances); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('58f37f51-15a2-4f58-9271-eb7e44e0de38', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 29, 'Which of the following best describes how CPT anesthesia codes are typically organized/selected (i.e., what determines which specific anesthesia code applies)?');
VALUES ('58f37f51-15a2-4f58-9271-eb7e44e0de38', '91ed282f-922a-4a63-ab3e-14a3df77bc11', 'Anesthesia codes map to procedure/anatomic-region categories (each with a defined base unit value), not a single universal code — correct code selection requires matching the anesthesia service to the procedure/region actually being supported.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions (base units, time units, physical status modifiers, qualifying circumstances); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('b39b1cff-abea-4ea5-b692-aad6df7593e6', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 30, 'Which of the following best describes the appropriate use of an anesthesia ''unusual anesthesia'' modifier or notation when general anesthesia is required for a procedure that doesn''t typically require it?');
VALUES ('b39b1cff-abea-4ea5-b692-aad6df7593e6', 'e846005b-d305-4f4e-bc4e-5e446ebebeb5', 'CPT anesthesia coding includes a mechanism (a specific modifier/notation convention) for flagging genuinely unusual anesthesia circumstances beyond a procedure''s typical anesthesia requirement — properly documented and coded, not treated identically to a routine case.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions (base units, time units, physical status modifiers, qualifying circumstances); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

-- ── Section: Surgery (30 questions) — Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('60d2a475-a061-4117-b2a7-6261c5906be9', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 31, 'The ''global surgical package'' concept in CPT surgery coding generally includes which of the following as part of the single surgical procedure code, not separately billable?');
VALUES ('60d2a475-a061-4117-b2a7-6261c5906be9', '2c061e5a-b333-459f-bb4b-8533785d6ed2', 'The global surgical package bundles routine, uncomplicated postoperative care within the defined global period (and surgeon-administered local/digital block anesthesia) into the single procedure code — a complication requiring a return to the OR, or an unrelated problem, are generally NOT included and may be separately reportable (often with an appropriate modifier).', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('c13f4c08-40ee-445f-8382-31c812c09d5b', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 32, 'Which modifier is generally used to indicate that a postoperative visit is unrelated to the original procedure and occurs during the postoperative (global) period?');
VALUES ('c13f4c08-40ee-445f-8382-31c812c09d5b', '9639f869-6ad8-45c1-8f2d-8001265e89a0', 'Modifier 24 specifically flags an E/M service, provided by the same physician during a postoperative global period, that is unrelated to the original surgery — allowing it to be separately reported despite falling within the global period.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('f8a07707-b3c4-4e50-9860-b7ac704baf3a', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 33, 'Which modifier indicates a return to the operating room for a related procedure during the postoperative period following the original surgery?');
VALUES ('f8a07707-b3c4-4e50-9860-b7ac704baf3a', '20836ee9-4f10-4e0f-bd4c-92e28fcb6ea0', 'Modifier 78 specifically identifies an unplanned return to the OR for a procedure related to the original surgery, occurring during the original procedure''s postoperative period — distinguishing it from modifier 79, which is for an unrelated procedure during the same postoperative period.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('37122886-625a-47a9-9238-319e3a55af69', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 34, 'Which modifier indicates an unrelated procedure performed by the same physician during the postoperative period of a prior surgery?');
VALUES ('37122886-625a-47a9-9238-319e3a55af69', '2c59d1e8-9b68-40b0-b151-063ac7c78629', 'Modifier 79 specifically flags an unrelated procedure/service by the same physician during another procedure''s postoperative period — the counterpart to modifier 78 (related return to OR).', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('53bc6c93-6b2e-4f4b-adc4-3c55b87d51b7', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 35, 'Which modifier indicates a staged or related procedure performed by the same physician during the postoperative period, planned prospectively or more extensive than the original procedure?');
VALUES ('53bc6c93-6b2e-4f4b-adc4-3c55b87d51b7', '7c4e9aea-2c1b-433d-a2ed-afb8c7258b97', 'Modifier 58 covers a staged/planned related procedure (or one that is more extensive than the original, or represents therapy following a diagnostic surgical procedure) performed by the same physician within the original procedure''s postoperative period.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('783c2854-14f0-4636-9a96-b776ac3f1677', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 36, 'A ''separate procedure'' designation in the CPT surgery section, appearing in parentheses after certain code descriptions, generally means:');
VALUES ('783c2854-14f0-4636-9a96-b776ac3f1677', '1d1a9d37-1603-489b-b561-994d62793ded', 'The ''separate procedure'' designation identifies codes that are typically bundled into more comprehensive procedures — separately reportable only when performed truly independently or unrelated to another procedure performed at the same session, often supported by modifier 59 or an appropriate X{EPSU} modifier when legitimately distinct.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('d833c376-d967-467f-9a71-4b4b577cd576', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 37, 'Which of the following best describes the general concept behind ''bundling'' in CPT surgical coding (independent of NCCI specifically)?');
VALUES ('d833c376-d967-467f-9a71-4b4b577cd576', '9ad68824-7655-46d6-90ff-411e2a832504', 'Bundling reflects that a comprehensive CPT code''s descriptor already encompasses certain integral component work — reporting those components separately in addition to the comprehensive code would overstate the actual distinct work performed.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('9ada821a-d693-4c62-be05-6b921971b833', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 38, 'Modifier 62 (''two surgeons'') is appropriately used when:');
VALUES ('9ada821a-d693-4c62-be05-6b921971b833', '4796bf70-d2c1-4dc5-86a1-5e49dd9b4503', 'Modifier 62 is specifically for two surgeons (often different specialties) each acting as a co-surgeon/primary surgeon for distinct portions of the same procedure — not for an assistant surgeon relationship (which uses modifier 80/81/82 conventions instead) or unrelated OR personnel.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('034f4ecd-3647-45ea-af48-e7ce8840125b', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 39, 'Which modifier is generally used to report an assistant surgeon''s involvement in a procedure?');
VALUES ('034f4ecd-3647-45ea-af48-e7ce8840125b', '7608d790-239f-4ebf-a90d-fe67cdb8855a', 'Modifier 80 (with related variants like 81 for minimum assistant and 82 for assistant surgeon when a qualified resident wasn''t available) specifically denotes an assistant-surgeon role, distinct from the co-surgeon concept covered by modifier 62.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('c6127e6e-1ade-4ca3-ab53-11b3deb4aa93', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 40, 'Which of the following best describes the coding concept behind a ''surgical tray'' or supply code in relation to a procedure''s global package?');
VALUES ('c6127e6e-1ade-4ca3-ab53-11b3deb4aa93', '2d4916b4-c925-4a99-9cca-fd7dd104e705', 'Routine supplies integral to a procedure are generally bundled into the global package, while certain distinct/itemizable supplies may be separately reportable via HCPCS Level II supply codes under specific payer policies — a nuanced, payer-dependent area rather than an absolute rule.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('a27cae26-4008-44c1-bdfa-ad77a9922969', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 41, 'Which of the following best describes correct use of modifier 22 (increased procedural services)?');
VALUES ('a27cae26-4008-44c1-bdfa-ad77a9922969', 'b2015381-2a7f-4087-8c73-0d4969b93162', 'Modifier 22 requires substantiating documentation showing genuinely increased complexity/effort beyond the typical case — it is not a routine or automatic modifier, and payers generally expect supporting documentation (e.g., an operative note narrative explaining the added difficulty) before recognizing it.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('dce3b8e5-d535-474d-affe-b357f6069a91', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 42, 'Which of the following best describes correct use of modifier 52 (reduced services)?');
VALUES ('dce3b8e5-d535-474d-affe-b357f6069a91', '55579065-59b2-4678-b09c-8a06262df2c3', 'Modifier 52 indicates a service was reduced/eliminated at the physician''s election (not due to an unexpected event) relative to the full code descriptor, when no more specific reduced-service code exists — distinct from modifier 53, which covers a procedure discontinued due to extenuating circumstances threatening patient well-being.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('f9b21ed8-a984-435d-a718-908cff07d27c', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 43, 'Which modifier indicates a surgical or diagnostic procedure was started but discontinued due to extenuating circumstances or those threatening the well-being of the patient?');
VALUES ('f9b21ed8-a984-435d-a718-908cff07d27c', 'f2587432-750c-409b-8aba-c0dd0b7d45f7', 'Modifier 53 is used when a procedure is discontinued after being started, due to circumstances threatening the patient''s well-being — modifiers 73/74 are the corresponding ASC-facility-specific variants (before/after anesthesia induction) used in that particular setting.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('8c5423d7-ee33-4ae2-b33b-de9ba0e3d4e3', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 44, 'Bilateral procedures in CPT coding, where a code descriptor does not already specify ''bilateral,'' are generally reported using which convention?');
VALUES ('8c5423d7-ee33-4ae2-b33b-de9ba0e3d4e3', 'eb15d4bf-9e5c-4fe5-9290-7091bc2bfa54', 'Modifier 50 is the standard convention for reporting a bilateral procedure using a code whose descriptor doesn''t already specify bilaterality — a foundational modifier concept distinct from the ''two surgeons'' or ''multiple procedures'' modifiers.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('1643edb9-5091-4dff-973f-ea9c8a36aee2', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 45, 'Which modifier is generally used to indicate multiple, distinct procedures (other than E/M services) performed during the same operative session by the same provider, often triggering a reduced-payment multiple-procedure fee schedule adjustment by the payer?');
VALUES ('1643edb9-5091-4dff-973f-ea9c8a36aee2', '50e34719-9a6b-4cd0-8d75-883c4e87f5e0', 'Modifier 51 flags multiple procedures performed at the same session, historically used to trigger payer multiple-procedure payment reduction logic for all but the primary/highest-valued procedure — some payers now apply this logic automatically without requiring the modifier, but understanding the underlying convention remains a core coding concept.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('5cbfda40-98ee-4db2-a28d-0d28563e973a', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 46, 'Which of the following best distinguishes modifier 59 (distinct procedural service) from the more specific X{EPSU} modifiers (XE, XS, XP, XU) CMS introduced?');
VALUES ('5cbfda40-98ee-4db2-a28d-0d28563e973a', '284f3fd2-2ee6-4eb0-a32a-cb238da90e43', 'CMS introduced the more specific X{EPSU} modifiers to reduce overuse/misuse of the broader modifier 59, while keeping modifier 59 valid for situations the more specific modifiers don''t clearly capture — understanding this relationship (not treating them as fully interchangeable or assuming 59 is retired) is a frequently tested nuance.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('3f0be64b-741a-442b-8698-e48b2a1cb8eb', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 47, 'Which of the following best describes an appropriate general use of modifier 59 or an X{EPSU} modifier, in the context of NCCI edits?');
VALUES ('3f0be64b-741a-442b-8698-e48b2a1cb8eb', 'e7854a2a-1395-4238-87a8-7bd2dfa4bf99', 'Modifier 59/X{EPSU} should reflect genuine clinical distinctness (different session, site, or truly separate procedure) — using it merely to bypass an NCCI edit for services that were, in fact, part of the same procedure is exactly the compliance misuse pattern these modifiers are meant to prevent, not enable.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('6bed366a-6794-4ac3-a958-0eee3f95b8cc', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 48, 'Which of the following best describes an ''unlisted procedure'' code in CPT?');
VALUES ('6bed366a-6794-4ac3-a958-0eee3f95b8cc', '92983329-8cbf-4a2a-a8cb-baf8002a6c6b', 'Unlisted procedure codes exist within each CPT section specifically for services lacking a more specific code — they require a special report (describing the procedure, why it was necessary, time/effort/equipment involved) to support payer review and payment determination, since there''s no standard fee schedule value tied to them.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('3779c5d0-d839-442a-b4c9-deab66ef7fe4', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 49, 'Which of the following best reflects the coding principle behind reporting a diagnostic endoscopy performed immediately before, and converted to, an open surgical procedure for the same condition at the same session?');
VALUES ('3779c5d0-d839-442a-b4c9-deab66ef7fe4', '7f0df236-6fd1-4783-acf5-8aab8510abef', 'When a diagnostic procedure directly leads to, and is converted into, a more extensive procedure at the same session for the same condition, the diagnostic component is generally bundled into the more extensive procedure — a bundling logic pattern that recurs across several CPT sections (endoscopy-to-open conversions being a classic example).', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('024040d5-79cb-4dbf-babb-ed60afc1c2e3', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 50, 'Which modifier indicates a procedure was performed on identical twins or one of a pair of similar organs (e.g., distinguishing left vs. right when the anatomic site itself must be specified but bilaterality is not being reported)?');
VALUES ('024040d5-79cb-4dbf-babb-ed60afc1c2e3', '612844f2-0421-495e-b857-baf3d7644711', 'LT/RT (HCPCS Level II anatomic modifiers commonly used alongside CPT codes) indicate which single side a unilateral procedure was performed on, distinct from modifier 50, which specifically indicates the SAME procedure was performed on BOTH sides during the same session.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('55b30400-aff1-4471-a7c3-bd1497c9211d', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 51, 'Which of the following best describes correct application of modifier 51 versus modifier 59 when multiple different procedures are performed at the same operative session?');
VALUES ('55b30400-aff1-4471-a7c3-bd1497c9211d', 'a41eec1d-1a7c-4b69-8ea9-e9ce5b7ca18d', 'These modifiers solve different problems: 51 is a general multiple-procedure payment-adjustment flag, while 59/X{EPSU} specifically addresses NCCI bundling-edit bypass for a genuinely distinct service — understanding they''re not interchangeable, and that applicability depends on the specific codes/edits involved, is a key surgery-coding nuance.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('ed1f51a7-5535-4d4e-b92f-851d48f96b5e', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 52, 'Which of the following best describes the general principle behind reporting an excision of a lesion together with a more extensive repair (e.g., a complex closure) of the resulting surgical defect?');
VALUES ('ed1f51a7-5535-4d4e-b92f-851d48f96b5e', '21d2f12d-9b9d-4971-8839-0857b2cfb174', 'This is a classic bundling-versus-separately-reportable distinction in integumentary surgery coding: simple closure is generally bundled into the excision, while genuinely more complex/extensive repair techniques may be separately reportable — matching documentation of the actual closure technique to the correct coding treatment is essential.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('7f90f181-0a43-4ba5-b611-2d9b1dacbf02', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 53, 'Which of the following best describes the coding principle behind an ''incidental appendectomy'' performed during an unrelated abdominal surgery, where the appendix was not diseased and removal was not the primary reason for surgery?');
VALUES ('7f90f181-0a43-4ba5-b611-2d9b1dacbf02', '97e7ed29-d07a-4d0c-864e-de143badebf8', 'An incidental procedure performed simply because access was already available (with no independent medical necessity/disease process addressed) is generally not separately codeable — a recurring surgical-coding compliance concept distinct from cases where a genuinely diseased or clinically indicated additional structure is addressed.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('049cf173-f2e9-4080-a8f2-51bfd8c1a1ec', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 54, 'Which of the following best describes the coding treatment of an intraoperative complication (e.g., an unintended enterotomy during a procedure) that the same surgeon repairs during the same operative session?');
VALUES ('049cf173-f2e9-4080-a8f2-51bfd8c1a1ec', 'fcb3fad2-f5b3-490f-8dae-0e9d2a9ca322', 'Repairing a complication caused during, and addressed within, the same operative session as the primary procedure is generally bundled into that procedure — separate reportability of complication management is a nuanced, guideline/payer-specific area, but the general default assumption is bundling absent specific supporting guidance.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('0d3277fe-299c-4053-8530-91f68a3d57ee', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 55, 'Which of the following best describes the coding concept of a ''component'' code being bundled into a more comprehensive code within the same surgical family, independent of any NCCI PTP edit specifically (i.e., as a matter of the code descriptors'' own inherent scope)?');
VALUES ('0d3277fe-299c-4053-8530-91f68a3d57ee', 'b1b6e2c8-46d2-4f86-ab08-530cfb52cf9c', 'Bundling logic is inherent to how many CPT code descriptors are actually written (a comprehensive code''s descriptor already encompassing certain component work) — NCCI edits are built to enforce/systematize this reality across claims processing, but the underlying bundling concept exists in the code descriptors themselves.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('a3ccfeae-6667-46c6-8fa7-b9fdc282b35f', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 56, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a planned, staged procedure (e.g., a second-stage reconstruction planned at the time of the first surgery) is performed during the global period of the first procedure?');
VALUES ('a3ccfeae-6667-46c6-8fa7-b9fdc282b35f', '458f940e-bba4-4b33-b146-b807fe6fe0fa', 'A planned, staged procedure during the original procedure''s global period is the classic use case for modifier 58 — distinct from modifier 78 (an UNplanned return to the OR for a related complication).', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('450dde5a-7bde-4f5d-9ecd-298eef5e092d', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 57, 'Which of the following best describes the coding principle for a diagnostic biopsy performed at the same session as, and immediately preceding, a more extensive excisional/therapeutic procedure addressing the same lesion/site based on the biopsy''s own intraoperative findings (e.g., frozen section guiding extent of resection)?');
VALUES ('450dde5a-7bde-4f5d-9ecd-298eef5e092d', '3c03eb7e-277a-4f3c-b1bb-2f38cba98c5d', 'Unlike some of the more clean-cut bundling examples elsewhere (e.g., screening-converted-to-diagnostic colonoscopy), biopsy-then-definitive-procedure-at-the-same-session scenarios are genuinely code/edit-specific and require checking the actual applicable NCCI edits and payer policy rather than assuming a single universal rule — an honest complexity worth flagging rather than oversimplifying.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('e93053ae-3d9c-4675-bb7f-08eab51d3f6b', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 58, 'Which of the following best describes the general principle behind CPT''s use of ''add-on'' codes specifically within the Surgery section (e.g., an add-on code for each additional lesion excised beyond the first, or each additional level of a spinal procedure)?');
VALUES ('e93053ae-3d9c-4675-bb7f-08eab51d3f6b', '8a5a00da-4db5-4c53-bf6b-c3e3f76d52c3', 'Surgical add-on codes commonly capture ''each additional'' increments (additional lesions, spinal levels, vessels, etc.) tied to a specific eligible primary code — reinforcing the general add-on code convention discussed earlier, applied specifically within Surgery.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('4f5d100c-8f79-4ef8-876d-f315ba220292', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 59, 'Which of the following best describes why accurately documenting laterality (left/right) and specific anatomic site (e.g., which specific finger, which specific vertebral level) matters for surgical CPT/HCPCS coding accuracy, beyond ICD-10-CM diagnosis coding?');
VALUES ('4f5d100c-8f79-4ef8-876d-f315ba220292', '03dc7cde-b6a1-487e-8956-8839ac148d0b', 'Anatomic specificity requirements aren''t unique to diagnosis coding — procedure coding (via anatomic modifiers, level-specific add-on codes, and even base code selection in some cases) often depends just as heavily on precise site/laterality documentation in the operative report.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('8e42ab76-21e8-46db-96ce-767347d5cf69', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 60, 'Which of the following best describes the coding treatment of converting a planned minimally invasive (e.g., laparoscopic) procedure to an open procedure due to intraoperative findings, with respect to which CPT code(s) should be reported?');
VALUES ('8e42ab76-21e8-46db-96ce-767347d5cf69', '79dd0743-f3f3-4b0f-b515-e62c616e96e0', 'This mirrors the ICD-10-PCS conversion principle discussed for CCS content: CPT coding for a converted procedure reports the approach actually completed (open), not the abandoned initial approach — a consistent cross-code-set coding logic worth recognizing.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions (global surgical package, separate procedure designation, bundling logic); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

-- ── Section: Radiology (10 questions) — Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions (professional/technical component split, contrast terminology); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('be857da1-0a3c-4bc9-8e03-0be42f1c7813', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 61, 'The distinction between the ''professional component'' (modifier 26) and ''technical component'' (modifier TC) in radiology coding reflects:');
VALUES ('be857da1-0a3c-4bc9-8e03-0be42f1c7813', 'e1ab0a37-6a9e-452b-b425-b81103d0cd91', 'The professional/technical component split is a core radiology (and some pathology/other diagnostic) coding concept — reflecting who performed which part of the service (interpretation vs. equipment/technologist/facility) when the two are not performed by the same entity/location.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions (professional/technical component split, contrast terminology); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('b5648b7d-ea14-4c5f-a6fa-5ef667c728a8', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 62, 'When the same physician or facility both owns the equipment and performs/interprets the imaging study, the appropriate coding approach is generally:');
VALUES ('b5648b7d-ea14-4c5f-a6fa-5ef667c728a8', 'cef56704-ee49-48c4-985a-5b96b0dd1027', 'When one entity performs both components, the global (unmodified) code is reported, representing the full combined service — splitting into 26/TC applies specifically when different entities perform the professional vs. technical portions.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions (professional/technical component split, contrast terminology); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('89c35b07-a8f4-4872-bc48-59344c04bc42', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 63, 'In radiology coding, the term ''contrast material'' in a code descriptor (e.g., ''with contrast,'' ''without contrast,'' ''without contrast followed by with contrast'') most directly affects:');
VALUES ('89c35b07-a8f4-4872-bc48-59344c04bc42', '4b1e7fe6-a031-4dd2-9dfb-41bf2fa229e9', 'Contrast use (none, with, or without-then-with) is frequently a defining element within a radiology code''s own descriptor — correct code selection requires matching the actual documented contrast protocol to the code descriptor, not treating contrast as a separately reportable add-on in isolation from code selection.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions (professional/technical component split, contrast terminology); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('c75c4056-b310-4f74-a95e-dd6c0dc58f74', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 64, 'Which of the following best describes the coding principle for a radiologist providing an official interpretation and report for a study, when the ordering physician also reviews the images informally as part of patient management?');
VALUES ('c75c4056-b310-4f74-a95e-dd6c0dc58f74', 'e35bcd15-2d1e-463c-8365-b7e6263d4610', 'Only one official, separately payable interpretation is generally supportable per study — an ordering physician''s clinical review of images as part of their own management of the patient doesn''t create a second billable professional-component interpretation of that same study.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions (professional/technical component split, contrast terminology); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('c7489c2b-e370-45b1-8984-3f1766dc73d7', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 65, 'Which best describes why radiology CPT codes for the same body part/modality (e.g., ''1 view,'' ''2 views,'' ''complete, minimum of 3 views'') often specify the number of views obtained?');
VALUES ('c7489c2b-e370-45b1-8984-3f1766dc73d7', '1f172bda-3c1c-42b6-87db-9c273c36fa6f', 'View count is a common, defining element of radiology code descriptors (especially for plain-film studies) — code selection must match the number of views actually documented as performed, not just the body part/modality.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions (professional/technical component split, contrast terminology); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('316abaa6-e2f6-4da1-a4be-bc02657b80ca', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 66, 'Which of the following best describes an appropriate use of modifier 26 in radiology billing?');
VALUES ('316abaa6-e2f6-4da1-a4be-bc02657b80ca', '0a517f46-969b-4ab2-98fe-0c87245d64b1', 'Modifier 26 is specifically for the professional-interpretation-only scenario where equipment ownership/technical performance belongs to a separate billing entity — not a general modifier tied to employment status or repeat-study scenarios.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions (professional/technical component split, contrast terminology); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('6d08ddce-67f1-4007-9683-4248b8faa3e7', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 67, 'Which best describes the coding treatment of a radiologic supervision and interpretation (S&I) code often reported alongside a separate surgical/procedural code for an image-guided procedure?');
VALUES ('6d08ddce-67f1-4007-9683-4248b8faa3e7', '701ad449-0caf-4a7a-97ae-d2bc5c164f16', 'Many image-guided procedures (e.g., certain injections, biopsies) require both a procedural code and a distinct S&I code to capture the full scope of work — coders need to recognize when a procedure''s code set structure calls for this two-code combination versus when imaging guidance is already bundled into a single combined code.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions (professional/technical component split, contrast terminology); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('e4c52755-e49e-4a37-be95-e1204145be42', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 68, 'Which of the following best describes why coders must pay close attention to whether a radiology code already includes guidance (e.g., ultrasound or fluoroscopic guidance) ''bundled'' into its descriptor versus requiring a separate guidance code?');
VALUES ('e4c52755-e49e-4a37-be95-e1204145be42', '83dae29f-1cb6-4f25-b746-029518d686ec', 'Whether imaging guidance is bundled into a specific procedural code''s own descriptor, or requires a separate add-on/guidance code, varies code by code — coders must check the specific code''s descriptor/instructional notes rather than applying a blanket assumption, since incorrectly double-reporting bundled guidance is a real compliance risk.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions (professional/technical component split, contrast terminology); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('a3495d92-4102-4b34-9868-ee7c575654bd', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 69, 'Which of the following best describes the general purpose of CPT codes for radiation oncology treatment planning and delivery (as distinguished from diagnostic radiology codes)?');
VALUES ('a3495d92-4102-4b34-9868-ee7c575654bd', '1e9adf12-7911-4653-8927-6c153074878f', 'Radiation oncology (therapeutic) coding reflects a fundamentally different, multi-step clinical workflow (planning, simulation, dosimetry, delivery, management) than a single diagnostic imaging study — recognizing this structural difference is a core radiology-section coding concept.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions (professional/technical component split, contrast terminology); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('b7ecc22d-c934-4e8b-9bbc-bf825643bee5', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 70, 'A CT scan of the abdomen and a CT scan of the pelvis, each performed with its own distinct acquisition and reportable under separate code descriptors, versus a single combined ''CT abdomen and pelvis'' code (where one exists), illustrates which coding principle?');
VALUES ('b7ecc22d-c934-4e8b-9bbc-bf825643bee5', 'e391c347-eee2-429d-962c-7d40071dc027', 'This mirrors the broader CPT-wide principle (also seen in Surgery and Pathology) that a single, more accurately descriptive combined code should be used over reporting separate component codes when that combined code genuinely reflects the service performed — unbundling into components when a combined code applies is a compliance-relevant coding error.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions (professional/technical component split, contrast terminology); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

-- ── Section: Pathology & Laboratory (10 questions) — Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions (panel definitions, qualitative/quantitative distinctions); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('c9bc9573-55ac-4735-b19a-385abcbb6c13', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 71, 'An ''organ or disease-oriented panel'' code (e.g., a basic metabolic panel or comprehensive metabolic panel) in CPT Pathology & Laboratory is used when:');
VALUES ('c9bc9573-55ac-4735-b19a-385abcbb6c13', 'f1a28b1e-b3f9-4a4b-954e-128fb652183e', 'Panel codes require that ALL of the specific component tests defined for that panel actually be performed — if even one defined component is missing, the panel code should generally not be reported; conversely, if all components of a defined panel were performed, reporting each one individually instead of the panel code would misrepresent (and generally overstate) the service.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions (panel definitions, qualitative/quantitative distinctions); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('f74bda9c-909b-4575-a383-0a44ed1d789c', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 72, 'If a physician orders all but one of the component tests that make up a defined CPT panel code, correct coding is generally to:');
VALUES ('f74bda9c-909b-4575-a383-0a44ed1d789c', '0f347b3e-bb7a-48a5-8efe-ae66346ec56d', 'Because panel codes are defined by their complete, specific component list, missing even one defined component means the panel code isn''t accurately supported — the actually-performed individual tests should be reported individually instead.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions (panel definitions, qualitative/quantitative distinctions); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('9f2250d2-4a1b-4e95-bd7f-a362d0651d2b', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 73, 'Which of the following best distinguishes a ''qualitative'' from a ''quantitative'' laboratory test result, as relevant to code selection?');
VALUES ('9f2250d2-4a1b-4e95-bd7f-a362d0651d2b', '658ed4f1-1890-424d-9ee8-072868020006', 'Qualitative (presence/absence) versus quantitative (specific measured amount) is a real, code-descriptor-relevant distinction in laboratory coding — using a quantitative code when only a qualitative result was actually performed (or vice versa) misrepresents the test performed.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions (panel definitions, qualitative/quantitative distinctions); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('bdce3b17-d8db-4686-a1c6-4628fb659587', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 74, 'Which of the following best describes the concept of a ''reflex test'' in laboratory coding/ordering, and its coding implication?');
VALUES ('bdce3b17-d8db-4686-a1c6-4628fb659587', 'c18329e3-a145-4501-8b2c-40bfd427ce80', 'Reflex testing describes a conditional, protocol-driven additional test performed only when an initial result meets a defined trigger — it''s separately codeable when it actually occurs (per the triggering condition and protocol/order), not a routine add-on for every specimen.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions (panel definitions, qualitative/quantitative distinctions); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('6038e4d3-26e7-41c7-8adc-bfcf7585bda1', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 75, 'Which of the following best describes a ''surgical pathology'' code level distinction (e.g., Level II through Level VI) in CPT?');
VALUES ('6038e4d3-26e7-41c7-8adc-bfcf7585bda1', '4f8dc632-1202-4f9d-a571-1a4bc814b025', 'Surgical pathology codes are tiered by complexity level, with each level''s CPT descriptor providing representative example specimen types — correct level selection depends on matching the actual specimen type/complexity to the appropriate level''s defined scope, not specimen size or submitting department alone.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions (panel definitions, qualitative/quantitative distinctions); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('e0d71619-f055-4e44-9641-670de8b46f17', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 76, 'Which of the following best describes the general coding principle when multiple specimens from the same patient are submitted for surgical pathology examination during the same encounter (e.g., three separate biopsy sites)?');
VALUES ('e0d71619-f055-4e44-9641-670de8b46f17', '36ecdbd3-4b99-4533-a4d7-6206ff9e13aa', 'Surgical pathology coding generally reflects the actual number of distinct specimens examined, each coded at its own appropriate level — this differs from the laboratory panel concept, where multiple individual analytes get consolidated into one code when a defined panel is fully performed.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions (panel definitions, qualitative/quantitative distinctions); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('a5b7173d-a173-4ef7-b6c0-115b50547f89', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 77, 'Which of the following best describes ''molecular pathology'' CPT codes, as a category distinct from routine chemistry/hematology lab codes?');
VALUES ('a5b7173d-a173-4ef7-b6c0-115b50547f89', '59b83809-8c9f-4877-9a71-cb4430abff6d', 'Molecular pathology codes address genetic/genomic-level testing (often coded per specific gene/analyte via Tier 1/Tier 2 code structures) — a structurally distinct coding approach from the panel/individual-analyte structure of traditional chemistry and hematology testing.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions (panel definitions, qualitative/quantitative distinctions); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('178e1c7c-71d9-49da-80ea-13c6658cab35', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 78, 'Which best describes the coding relevance of whether a laboratory test was performed by the reporting physician''s own office lab (in-house) versus sent to an outside reference laboratory?');
VALUES ('178e1c7c-71d9-49da-80ea-13c6658cab35', 'b4087ba3-1775-474b-981c-aa7097b1cfb6', 'Whether a test was performed in-house versus by an outside reference lab affects which entity should bill for it, and modifier 90 exists specifically to flag that a billing physician is reporting a test actually performed by an outside reference laboratory — accurately reflecting who performed the work.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions (panel definitions, qualitative/quantitative distinctions); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('c33a44e9-c7c2-4dab-8f15-9395a5e0dd8e', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 79, 'Which of the following best describes the coding treatment when a laboratory test is performed but the specimen is deemed inadequate/unsatisfactory for interpretation, requiring the physician to request a new specimen?');
VALUES ('c33a44e9-c7c2-4dab-8f15-9395a5e0dd8e', '9fbb73bc-404c-4b81-b7da-41ea0bfcd3d5', 'An unsatisfactory specimen scenario is distinct from a modifier-91 repeat test (which is for a valid initial result followed by a medically necessary NEW result) — coders should recognize this is a different situation requiring attention to what was actually accomplished and applicable payer policy, not a one-size-fits-all rule.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions (panel definitions, qualitative/quantitative distinctions); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('599ecae5-a9b7-41ad-a6ab-04b727cc76b1', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 80, 'Which of the following best describes the difference between a ''consultation'' in surgical pathology (e.g., a second-opinion review of slides prepared by another pathologist/facility) and the primary surgical pathology examination itself?');
VALUES ('599ecae5-a9b7-41ad-a6ab-04b727cc76b1', '35b985e3-4eac-4ead-927d-34631ca9fd73', 'Pathology consultation codes (for reviewing outside/prior material, e.g., a second opinion) are a distinct code family from the primary surgical pathology examination codes — reflecting a genuinely different service (review of existing material vs. original examination).', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions (panel definitions, qualitative/quantitative distinctions); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

-- ── Section: Medicine (10 questions) — Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions (immunization administration vs. vaccine product coding, therapy service concepts); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('69a61214-bf8e-4db9-8474-7a5d95ff162c', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 81, 'Which of the following best describes the CPT coding principle for administering a vaccine/immunization?');
VALUES ('69a61214-bf8e-4db9-8474-7a5d95ff162c', '27670894-c2b2-4b22-8f85-5bbd15fea10a', 'Vaccine coding is a well-known two-component structure — the vaccine product code and the administration code are reported together to fully capture both the product given and the service of administering it, a distinction commonly missed by those assuming a single all-in-one code.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions (immunization administration vs. vaccine product coding, therapy service concepts); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('b90211e5-55b4-4e74-9d9d-a58d1475a6ba', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 82, 'Which of the following best describes the coding distinction between ''physical therapy'' timed codes and untimed (service-based) codes within the CPT Medicine section''s rehabilitation-related codes?');
VALUES ('b90211e5-55b4-4e74-9d9d-a58d1475a6ba', '0f107731-db28-4f66-adf1-b9aeac8ef19a', 'The timed-versus-untimed distinction (and associated documentation/unit-counting rules, such as the widely used ''8-minute rule'' concept in many timed-code contexts) is a core Medicine-section rehabilitation coding concept — untimed codes are reported once regardless of duration, while timed codes require duration documentation to support the number of units billed.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions (immunization administration vs. vaccine product coding, therapy service concepts); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('fc192365-78f4-4485-849c-de56e1106daa', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 83, 'Which of the following best describes psychiatric diagnostic evaluation codes versus psychotherapy codes within the CPT Medicine section?');
VALUES ('fc192365-78f4-4485-849c-de56e1106daa', 'd33d047f-aaa6-48c7-92b6-1460cae6d3e9', 'Psychiatric diagnostic evaluation and psychotherapy are distinct code families for distinct service types (initial/periodic assessment vs. ongoing time-based therapeutic treatment) — conflating them, or assuming psychotherapy must be reported via E/M codes, misrepresents the Medicine section''s actual structure.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions (immunization administration vs. vaccine product coding, therapy service concepts); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('ef6a77cb-5fe4-4b15-9f08-a499ab970d06', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 84, 'Which of the following best describes ''add-on'' psychotherapy codes reported alongside an E/M service on the same date (e.g., for medication management combined with psychotherapy)?');
VALUES ('ef6a77cb-5fe4-4b15-9f08-a499ab970d06', 'b88243b4-8a07-4b8e-9c21-e191f655b364', 'CPT includes specific add-on psychotherapy codes designed to be reported together with a same-date E/M service — reflecting the real clinical practice of combining medication management (E/M) with a therapy session in one visit, a structural nuance of the Medicine section.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions (immunization administration vs. vaccine product coding, therapy service concepts); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('8d1ded90-6255-4711-9a00-2d4e4b4d9932', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 85, 'Which of the following best describes cardiovascular stress test coding within the Medicine section (e.g., a treadmill stress test) with respect to its components?');
VALUES ('8d1ded90-6255-4711-9a00-2d4e4b4d9932', '7b64a09c-ca13-4c33-84e7-8b95128b2286', 'Cardiovascular diagnostic testing in the Medicine section can involve a similar professional/technical component logic to radiology, depending on the specific code and who performed which portion — recognizing this parallel concept (rather than assuming every Medicine-section code is a single indivisible unit) matters for accurate coding.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions (immunization administration vs. vaccine product coding, therapy service concepts); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('da0d1800-7c31-4ff0-b5ae-a979f959d92b', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 86, 'Which of the following best describes the general purpose of the Medicine section''s ''Special Services, Procedures and Reports'' codes (e.g., codes for after-hours services or handling/conveyance of a specimen)?');
VALUES ('da0d1800-7c31-4ff0-b5ae-a979f959d92b', '4905bed3-6cd6-44fb-b581-a146471a3afe', 'This code family captures ancillary circumstances (after-hours care, specimen handling, unusual travel, etc.) reported in addition to a primary service — not stand-alone primary procedures themselves, a distinction relevant to correctly sequencing/pairing these codes with the actual primary service rendered.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions (immunization administration vs. vaccine product coding, therapy service concepts); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('dbd710dd-c8d5-4404-b8f4-eef97ae81447', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 87, 'Which of the following best describes the coding concept for reporting an immunization administered without any accompanying face-to-face physician E/M service that day (e.g., a nurse-only immunization visit)?');
VALUES ('dbd710dd-c8d5-4404-b8f4-eef97ae81447', '74319ff8-206b-4eff-aedf-9d509655b7a4', 'Vaccine product and administration codes stand on their own and don''t require an accompanying E/M code — reporting an E/M service that wasn''t actually separately performed just to ''support'' the immunization visit would itself be an inaccurate/unsupported coding practice.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions (immunization administration vs. vaccine product coding, therapy service concepts); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('87bf79d0-893e-4b3e-95d9-15402eedef23', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 88, 'Which of the following best describes the coding concept behind ''chronic care management'' (CCM) or similar care-management service codes within the Medicine section (or, depending on the code, E/M-adjacent codes)?');
VALUES ('87bf79d0-893e-4b3e-95d9-15402eedef23', '19662833-2ff1-4c45-8c49-c2627c3992cd', 'Care management service codes (CCM and related concepts) specifically capture non-face-to-face coordination time/activities for complex chronic-condition patients, governed by defined time thresholds and documentation requirements — a structurally distinct concept from a traditional face-to-face visit code.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions (immunization administration vs. vaccine product coding, therapy service concepts); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('de9c8c2d-9e9b-4d93-9f98-d960c230eaa3', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 89, 'Which of the following best describes the coding treatment of allergy testing versus allergen immunotherapy (allergy shots) within the CPT Medicine section?');
VALUES ('de9c8c2d-9e9b-4d93-9f98-d960c230eaa3', '4596a50e-5835-4747-960a-ec2397ed8c67', 'Allergy testing and allergen immunotherapy administration are distinct Medicine-section code families reflecting genuinely different clinical services (diagnostic testing vs. ongoing desensitization treatment) — not interchangeable or identical code sets.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions (immunization administration vs. vaccine product coding, therapy service concepts); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('4b48080b-19c5-47c2-8cc5-adb8df9d7555', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 90, 'Which of the following best describes the coding principle behind reporting supply of an allergen immunotherapy extract/vial separately from the injection administration service itself?');
VALUES ('4b48080b-19c5-47c2-8cc5-adb8df9d7555', '1f9eed3f-5dac-4fed-96fb-88ec9ec8fdb7', 'This mirrors the vaccine product/administration split concept — immunotherapy antigen preparation/provision and the administration (injection) service can be separately identified/coded, reflecting genuinely distinct components of the overall service.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions (immunization administration vs. vaccine product coding, therapy service concepts); not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

-- ── Section: Modifiers & NCCI (10 questions) — Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions and CMS NCCI Procedure-to-Procedure/MUE edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('e7dbb161-cd2a-4bda-9fcc-a1fd80dfb6f6', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 91, 'NCCI Procedure-to-Procedure (PTP) edits are designed primarily to:');
VALUES ('e7dbb161-cd2a-4bda-9fcc-a1fd80dfb6f6', 'f87c250f-1150-49fa-ac7d-93d445aa6820', 'PTP edits are CMS''s mechanism for catching code pairs that shouldn''t normally be billed together for the same encounter (component/comprehensive relationships, or mutually exclusive procedures) — applicable across professional and outpatient facility claims, not just inpatient.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions and CMS NCCI Procedure-to-Procedure/MUE edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('02fb6d58-7acd-4f3f-a8b0-fe0f1a973073', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 92, 'A Medically Unlikely Edit (MUE) is best described as:');
VALUES ('02fb6d58-7acd-4f3f-a8b0-fe0f1a973073', 'a94db366-5ea0-4154-adb0-076b33fc73a4', 'MUEs set a per-code, per-day unit ceiling representing the maximum units clinically plausible for one patient on one date — a different edit type/purpose than PTP edits, which govern code-PAIR relationships rather than single-code unit limits.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions and CMS NCCI Procedure-to-Procedure/MUE edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('7dce2333-5763-4fc7-96e2-bad0c4dc5dbf', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 93, 'Which of the following best describes an NCCI PTP edit''s ''modifier indicator'' of ''0'' (sometimes called a ''non-allowed'' or non-modifiable edit)?');
VALUES ('7dce2333-5763-4fc7-96e2-bad0c4dc5dbf', 'fe6ae110-23e4-4233-b996-d4a468174025', 'PTP edits carry a modifier indicator: ''1'' means an appropriate modifier CAN bypass the edit when clinically justified, while ''0'' means the edit cannot be bypassed by any modifier — understanding this distinction prevents inappropriately attempting to force payment on a genuinely non-bypassable edit.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions and CMS NCCI Procedure-to-Procedure/MUE edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('abee760e-b68f-42b2-a19c-02cc1a6f0ef4', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 94, 'Which of the following best describes appropriate modifier use to bypass an NCCI PTP edit with a modifier indicator of ''1''?');
VALUES ('abee760e-b68f-42b2-a19c-02cc1a6f0ef4', 'c91fd678-b927-4c23-87cb-669d3db50547', 'A ''1'' modifier indicator means bypass is POSSIBLE when clinically justified — it is not an invitation to apply a bypass modifier reflexively to any denied claim; genuine clinical distinctness must actually exist and be documented.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions and CMS NCCI Procedure-to-Procedure/MUE edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('67ea3df5-b4e4-4989-a00c-1d446d296713', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 95, 'Which of the following best describes ''add-on code'' edits within the NCCI framework?');
VALUES ('67ea3df5-b4e4-4989-a00c-1d446d296713', '297a8bf6-4184-40d6-9277-fe57b3114f1a', 'NCCI-related add-on code edit logic reinforces the basic CPT add-on code convention itself — an add-on code reported without an eligible primary code present on the same claim doesn''t reflect a valid billing scenario under the add-on code''s own defined structure.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions and CMS NCCI Procedure-to-Procedure/MUE edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('dd08f724-560e-451e-96c2-2b223155f0f4', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 96, 'Which of the following best reflects a compliance-relevant reason coders should understand NCCI edits, beyond simply getting claims paid?');
VALUES ('dd08f724-560e-451e-96c2-2b223155f0f4', '48a42f95-7666-444b-babe-79a6a5ffbbd3', 'Just like other coding-accuracy compliance concepts, systematically forcing NCCI edit bypasses without genuine clinical support for distinctness is a real fraud/abuse exposure pattern — NCCI understanding is a compliance competency, not merely a claims-processing mechanic.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions and CMS NCCI Procedure-to-Procedure/MUE edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('2f32be9e-a60f-4d5c-8947-1a13b3912e3c', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 97, 'Which of the following best describes modifier 91 (repeat clinical diagnostic laboratory test)?');
VALUES ('2f32be9e-a60f-4d5c-8947-1a13b3912e3c', '02e37fdb-dcc0-4a04-a1ba-62097ab296ac', 'Modifier 91 specifically covers medically necessary repeat lab testing reflecting a genuine need to obtain a new result (e.g., monitoring a changing clinical value) — not a repeat due to technical/equipment problems (which wouldn''t be separately billed) or simple confirmation of an already-valid result.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions and CMS NCCI Procedure-to-Procedure/MUE edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('285453ba-dfa7-48eb-84e9-1cc1195ea0b2', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 98, 'Which of the following best describes modifier 76 (repeat procedure or service by same physician or other qualified health care professional)?');
VALUES ('285453ba-dfa7-48eb-84e9-1cc1195ea0b2', '7dfd9d79-2af4-4c18-9bb0-736a84c4c629', 'Modifier 76 clarifies that an apparently duplicate procedure code on the same date genuinely reflects the same physician repeating that specific procedure (a real, distinct clinical event), not a billing error — modifier 77 is the parallel modifier when a DIFFERENT physician performs the repeat.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions and CMS NCCI Procedure-to-Procedure/MUE edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('c62c58f9-d583-40ec-b620-70c712d7f2f1', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 99, 'Which of the following best describes the general purpose of HCPCS Level II codes in relation to CPT (Level I) codes?');
VALUES ('c62c58f9-d583-40ec-b620-70c712d7f2f1', '81010a10-b437-47ac-8654-3015cc0e28fc', 'HCPCS Level II fills gaps CPT (Level I) doesn''t cover — DME, certain drugs/biologicals, supplies, and other items — using its own alphanumeric coding structure, working alongside rather than replacing CPT for the services CPT does cover.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions and CMS NCCI Procedure-to-Procedure/MUE edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('65ca08ed-56e9-4033-9f09-c6b343527108', '84a10098-66b1-4504-b7be-eb8c60f6e0c9', 100, 'Which of the following best describes a coder''s general responsibility when a claim scrubber/clearinghouse system flags a potential NCCI edit or MUE violation before submission?');
VALUES ('65ca08ed-56e9-4033-9f09-c6b343527108', '88a8dff0-c834-4c04-a8af-146e3895bb32', 'A system-generated compliance flag should prompt actual documentation review to determine the correct resolution — neither automatic override (bypass modifier applied reflexively) nor automatic dismissal is the compliant response; the underlying clinical facts should drive the correction.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions and CMS NCCI Procedure-to-Procedure/MUE edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');
